1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,360
The previous lesson found an object
orientation.

2
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:07,900
We now calculate a connection from that object
to another location.

3
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:13,420
This chapter has two episodes. First, choose
circle and line representations to generate

4
00:00:13,660 --> 00:00:18,660
and test points. Next, enclose the target size
and rebuild its bounds after rotation.

5
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:24,200
What numbers let a program describe a
targeting line or an object size?

6
00:00:24,540 --> 00:00:28,160
We will find origins and directions in
grappling footage,

7
00:00:28,160 --> 00:00:30,720
then distinguish lines, segments and rays.

8
00:00:31,020 --> 00:00:33,040
Next we will represent round objects with
spheres

9
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:36,260
and calculate a box enclosing a set of points.

10
00:00:36,600 --> 00:00:39,820
Finally we will rotate a box, expose a common
error,

11
00:00:39,820 --> 00:00:42,400
and derive a safe transformation formula.

12
00:00:44,816 --> 00:00:46,450
We travel among rocks in A Story About My
Uncle.

13
00:00:46,670 --> 00:00:48,890
Watch the bright connection extending from the
hand.

14
00:00:50,266 --> 00:00:55,506
When it appears, distinguish its origin near
the hand from its attachment on a rock.

15
00:00:56,862 --> 00:01:00,162
Aiming at another rock changes the connection
direction.

16
00:01:00,482 --> 00:01:03,662
One starting point alone does not determine a
line.

17
00:01:04,938 --> 00:01:08,378
Its screen length depends on the camera and
perspective;

18
00:01:08,458 --> 00:01:11,358
pixels are not automatically world distance.

19
00:01:12,654 --> 00:01:15,974
Remember how the endpoints relate as we move.

20
00:01:15,974 --> 00:01:19,394
Our diagram will calculate that relation in
explicitly chosen coordinates.

21
00:01:20,930 --> 00:01:22,890
The visible grapple motivates the mathematics;

22
00:01:23,370 --> 00:01:27,350
it does not reveal this game ray tests or
physics implementation.

23
00:01:28,050 --> 00:01:32,190
With the grapple's endpoints identified, we
need a numerical line. Before choosing

24
00:01:32,370 --> 00:01:38,010
its formula, use the familiar circle from
earlier lessons to separate three tasks.

25
00:01:38,210 --> 00:01:44,470
Do we store the shape, generate a point on it,
or test a supplied point? After distinguishing

26
00:01:44,710 --> 00:01:48,870
these tasks for the circle, we will apply the
same questions to the connection line.

27
00:01:49,583 --> 00:01:53,043
We can describe the same circle differently
depending on the calculation.

28
00:01:53,263 --> 00:01:57,143
Storing its center and radius gives direct
shape data.

29
00:01:57,403 --> 00:02:01,223
A parametric representation constructs a point
by adding

30
00:02:01,223 --> 00:02:05,063
radius times cosine and sine of an angle to
the center.

31
00:02:05,243 --> 00:02:08,443
An implicit representation compares squared
distance

32
00:02:08,443 --> 00:02:11,783
from a candidate point to the center with
squared radius.

33
00:02:11,843 --> 00:02:15,523
Equality means the boundary; a smaller squared
distance means the interior.

34
00:02:15,703 --> 00:02:18,523
Watch the classification change as the point
moves.

35
00:02:18,523 --> 00:02:23,023
Parametric equations conveniently construct
points;

36
00:02:23,183 --> 00:02:25,423
implicit equations conveniently test them.
Neither is always better.

37
00:02:25,623 --> 00:02:28,563
An angle parameter does not automatically
represent time.

38
00:02:28,723 --> 00:02:32,443
Define whether a parameter means angle, time
or distance.

39
00:02:33,083 --> 00:02:37,863
For a circle at the origin, x squared plus y
squared equals radius squared.

40
00:02:38,143 --> 00:02:40,403
Translating its center changes the equation.

41
00:02:40,623 --> 00:02:46,703
Take center two, three and radius one.

42
00:02:47,103 --> 00:02:50,563
Subtract two from x and three from y before
squaring.

43
00:02:50,763 --> 00:02:56,863
Expansion gives x squared plus y squared minus
four x minus six y plus twelve equal to zero.

44
00:02:57,143 --> 00:02:59,583
A general conic therefore includes linear and

45
00:02:59,583 --> 00:03:02,523
constant terms as well as squared and cross
terms.

46
00:03:02,523 --> 00:03:05,723
A circle needs two center components and one
radius.

47
00:03:06,063 --> 00:03:08,623
A sphere needs three center components and one
radius.

48
00:03:08,863 --> 00:03:12,343
Different radii along the axes describe an
ellipse or ellipsoid.

49
00:03:12,623 --> 00:03:16,023
Nonuniformly stretching a sphere generally
changes it into an ellipsoid.

50
00:03:16,650 --> 00:03:19,390
For the circle, center and radius store the
shape,

51
00:03:19,390 --> 00:03:22,890
angle generates a point, and distance tests
inside or outside.

52
00:03:23,210 --> 00:03:27,330
Replace the circle with two connection points
and apply the same three questions to a line.

53
00:03:28,300 --> 00:03:34,953
This is an official Portal 2 preview
demonstration. Red follows the beam

54
00:03:35,213 --> 00:03:38,666
from its origin to the cube. Moving the cube
changes the outgoing direction.

55
00:03:39,679 --> 00:03:45,711
Here is a different excerpt. A beam aimed at
the receiver changes its indicator

56
00:03:46,702 --> 00:03:49,622
and opens the route. Connect the origin,
direction change and visible result.

57
00:03:50,653 --> 00:03:57,580
These are projected screen observations; the
engine method and world

58
00:03:58,987 --> 00:04:02,167
distances are unverified. Next, identify both
endpoints of a grapple as well.

59
00:04:02,967 --> 00:04:06,127
We connect to another rock and travel through
the air.

60
00:04:06,347 --> 00:04:11,429
Find both endpoints whenever the beam appears.

61
00:04:11,769 --> 00:04:13,637
Subtracting origin from attachment gives a

62
00:04:13,637 --> 00:04:16,309
displacement vector containing direction and
length.

63
00:04:18,791 --> 00:04:23,451
Even when two rocks lie in the same direction,
their displacement lengths differ.

64
00:04:25,833 --> 00:04:28,333
For direction alone, divide displacement by
its length.

65
00:04:28,493 --> 00:04:31,993
Store a unit direction and distance
separately.

66
00:04:34,255 --> 00:04:39,695
The hand-to-rock connection and the character
actual path are different questions.

67
00:04:39,895 --> 00:04:43,535
Forces and trajectories belong to later
dynamics lessons.

68
00:04:45,997 --> 00:04:48,717
Focus here on geometric descriptions between
positions.

69
00:04:48,753 --> 00:04:54,197
A fractional parameter and a distance
parameter have different meanings.

70
00:04:54,817 --> 00:04:56,637
This is a different excerpt from the earlier
grapple.

71
00:04:57,565 --> 00:05:01,665
When the hand connects to a rock, red joins
the two observed endpoints.

72
00:05:02,753 --> 00:05:06,293
Selecting the next rock changes the endpoint
and direction.

73
00:05:06,533 --> 00:05:11,241
Do not treat the blue effect and the body
travel path as the same straight line.

74
00:05:12,430 --> 00:05:17,030
The segment joining origin and attachment is
our defined calculation connection.

75
00:05:17,310 --> 00:05:20,778
The body travels through space while the
camera follows it.

76
00:05:21,886 --> 00:05:27,990
We now move to the later excerpt. Observe
release and arrival near the rock.

77
00:05:27,990 --> 00:05:32,813
Generating intermediate connection points
requires the next parameter formula.

78
00:05:33,433 --> 00:05:37,673
Call the origin the letter o and displacement
delta.

79
00:05:37,993 --> 00:05:41,173
Position is the origin plus t times delta.

80
00:05:41,513 --> 00:05:44,613
Zero gives the origin and one gives the
endpoint.

81
00:05:44,873 --> 00:05:47,953
Restricting t to zero through one defines the
segment.

82
00:05:48,253 --> 00:05:52,133
Allowing every real t produces an infinite
line.

83
00:05:52,453 --> 00:05:54,453
Negative t extends in the opposite direction.

84
00:05:54,633 --> 00:05:57,573
A mathematical ray usually restricts t to

85
00:05:57,653 --> 00:05:59,513
nonnegative values and extends infinitely
forward.

86
00:05:59,853 --> 00:06:03,093
The book also calls a finite directed segment
a ray. Engine ray queries

87
00:06:03,093 --> 00:06:08,013
may have a maximum distance, so inspect the
actual allowed domain.

88
00:06:08,153 --> 00:06:12,613
Record the origin, direction and parameter
range together,

89
00:06:12,793 --> 00:06:15,433
rather than relying on a name.

90
00:06:16,117 --> 00:06:21,357
Let the origin be two, one and displacement
six, three. Where is the midpoint?

91
00:06:22,097 --> 00:06:27,737
At t equal to one half, add three, one point
five. The point is five, two point five.

92
00:06:28,057 --> 00:06:34,417
This one half is a fraction of the segment,
not half a second or half a meter.

93
00:06:34,717 --> 00:06:36,977
The displacement length is square root of
forty-five.

94
00:06:37,137 --> 00:06:40,717
Divide displacement by that length to obtain a
unit direction.

95
00:06:41,337 --> 00:06:45,977
Multiplying that unit direction by s makes s a
distance.

96
00:06:46,117 --> 00:06:49,617
The same midpoint uses s equal to half of
square root forty-five.

97
00:06:49,937 --> 00:06:54,217
Identical endpoints give zero displacement.
Check before normalization

98
00:06:54,217 --> 00:06:57,057
and define whether to treat the degenerate
input as a point or reject it.

99
00:06:57,683 --> 00:07:02,823
Our origin is two, one and endpoint eight,
four. Displacement six,

100
00:07:02,823 --> 00:07:07,903
three has length square root forty-five.
Define the coordinate unit as meters.

101
00:07:08,263 --> 00:07:12,343
Multiplying by t one half adds three meters
and one point five meters,

102
00:07:12,343 --> 00:07:17,063
reaching five meters, two point five meters.
The parameter t has no meter unit.

103
00:07:17,403 --> 00:07:21,003
Divide the same displacement by square root
forty-five for a unit direction.

104
00:07:21,223 --> 00:07:26,283
Multiply it by distance square root forty-five
over two to reach the identical midpoint.

105
00:07:26,283 --> 00:07:28,983
Fraction and distance give the same point.
Next,

106
00:07:29,563 --> 00:07:33,663
check whether slope can represent every line
direction.

107
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:39,626
In another connection, identify the two red
endpoints afresh.

108
00:07:39,826 --> 00:07:43,306
A new rock requires a new origin and
displacement.

109
00:07:44,328 --> 00:07:48,688
The calculation rule remains: t zero is the
origin and t one the attachment.

110
00:07:49,491 --> 00:07:53,451
Screen travel speed is not itself t or a
distance in meters.

111
00:07:54,414 --> 00:07:57,874
In the following excerpt, observe connection,

112
00:07:58,749 --> 00:08:01,309
travel and landing in order. Hide the line
when the hand or rock is obscured.

113
00:08:02,283 --> 00:08:08,758
Our earlier numbers were a fixed-coordinate
example, not measurements from this game.

114
00:08:08,758 --> 00:08:11,973
Next, find a representation that also handles
vertical lines.

115
00:08:12,733 --> 00:08:15,553
We aim at rocks above and below us.

116
00:08:15,913 --> 00:08:19,133
The beam looks vertical in some views and
diagonal in others.

117
00:08:21,713 --> 00:08:23,693
Both are ordinary lines, yet a slope-only

118
00:08:24,153 --> 00:08:27,673
representation has a problem at vertical
lines.

119
00:08:27,993 --> 00:08:35,313
When the x change is zero, we cannot divide
the y change by it.

120
00:08:37,973 --> 00:08:42,333
Our diagram will use a normal and offset
instead.

121
00:08:42,673 --> 00:08:45,273
A normal is perpendicular to the line
direction.

122
00:08:48,133 --> 00:08:50,793
Screen vertical comes from the camera. For
calculation,

123
00:08:50,853 --> 00:08:55,933
first choose the coordinate frame defining x
and y.

124
00:08:58,373 --> 00:09:01,813
We will also find points equidistant from two
sites.

125
00:09:02,273 --> 00:09:06,113
Their connecting line and perpendicular
bisector are different lines.

126
00:09:06,733 --> 00:09:11,293
The slope division failed for a vertical line.
In a separate task from joining the two sites,

127
00:09:11,293 --> 00:09:15,453
find points equally distant from zero, zero
and two, zero.

128
00:09:15,773 --> 00:09:19,813
Equal distance is the question that produces a
perpendicular bisector.

129
00:09:20,433 --> 00:09:25,893
For a point x, y, the squared distances are x
squared plus y squared,

130
00:09:25,893 --> 00:09:29,513
and x minus two squared plus y squared.

131
00:09:29,813 --> 00:09:34,153
Equate and expand them. Common terms cancel,
leaving four x equals four,

132
00:09:34,153 --> 00:09:38,433
so x equals one. Draw this vertical line
between the two points.

133
00:09:38,913 --> 00:09:42,213
The next formula calls the perpendicular
direction a normal.

134
00:09:42,453 --> 00:09:46,553
A dot multiplies matching entries and adds
them: two,

135
00:09:46,553 --> 00:09:51,073
zero dotted with x, y gives two x. This
expresses the same vertical line.

136
00:09:51,700 --> 00:09:55,860
The familiar slope-intercept equation needs a
different form,

137
00:09:56,140 --> 00:09:59,820
x equal to a constant, for a vertical line.

138
00:10:00,020 --> 00:10:02,900
Using normal n dotted with a point equal to
offset d

139
00:10:02,900 --> 00:10:06,460
represents vertical and horizontal lines in
one form.

140
00:10:06,700 --> 00:10:11,640
The normal is perpendicular to the line. A
zero normal does not define a valid line.

141
00:10:11,960 --> 00:10:15,640
For points equidistant from zero, zero and
two, zero,

142
00:10:15,900 --> 00:10:21,180
use displacement two, zero as normal and
midpoint one, zero on the bisector.

143
00:10:21,180 --> 00:10:27,120
The equation is two x equals two, or x equals
one. This vertical line bisects the two sites.

144
00:10:27,300 --> 00:10:33,160
When normalizing n, divide d by the same
length. Scaling only n moves the line;

145
00:10:33,440 --> 00:10:37,600
the residual is directly a signed distance
only for a unit normal.

146
00:10:38,233 --> 00:10:41,933
Choose a circle or line representation for the
task,

147
00:10:42,073 --> 00:10:45,533
and compare a segment fraction with a ray
distance at the same point.

148
00:10:45,913 --> 00:10:51,213
We can now construct the connection. Contact
also needs the target size.

149
00:10:51,493 --> 00:10:54,193
The next episode encloses that object in
spheres and boxes

150
00:10:54,193 --> 00:10:57,293
and calculates how its bounds change under
rotation.
